DAY 12 – ADVANCED LOOPS

1. FOR LOOP WITH ELSE

The else block runs only when the for loop completes
normally without encountering a break.

If the loop is terminated using break, the else block
will not execute.


2. WHILE LOOP WITH ELSE

The else block runs when the while loop completes
normally without encountering a break.

If break is used, the else block will not execute.


3. BREAK STATEMENT

The break statement is used to exit a loop before
the loop completes.

It is commonly used when a specific condition is met
and further iteration is not required.


4. CONTINUE STATEMENT

The continue statement skips the remaining statements
of the current iteration and moves to the next iteration.

It does not terminate the complete loop.


5. ASSERT KEYWORD

The assert keyword is used to perform debugging checks
during development.

It checks whether a given condition is True.

If the condition is False, Python raises AssertionError.

Syntax:

assert condition

or

assert condition, "Error message"


6. USE CASES OF ASSERT

Assert can be useful for:

• Unit testing
• Development and debugging
• Validating conditions during development

Assertions are not recommended for production validation
because they can be disabled using Python's -O option.


7. BREAK VS CONTINUE VS ASSERT

break → Exits the loop early.

continue → Skips the current iteration.

assert → Checks whether a condition is True and raises
AssertionError when it is False.


KEY TAKEAWAY

for/while with else → else executes when the loop
completes without break.

break → Stops the loop.

continue → Skips the current iteration.

assert → Checks a condition during development.